427 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText ( Local Variables)
/Alt ()
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
428 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Subprograms can define their own variables, thereby defining local referencing environments. Variables that are defined inside subprograms are called local variables, because their scope is usually the body of the subprogram in which they are defined)
/Alt ()
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
429 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 803 0 R
/ActualText (local variables can be either static or stack dynamic)
/Alt ()
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
430 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 804 0 R
/ActualText (Static Instances)
/Alt ()
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
431 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 805 0 R
/ActualText (Definition: Memory for static instances is allocated at compile time and persists for the lifetime of the program.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
432 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 806 0 R
/ActualText (Scope: They can be global, local \(within a function but declared static\), or class-level \(static members\).)
/Alt ()
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
433 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 807 0 R
/ActualText (Lifetime: They exist until the program terminates.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
434 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 808 0 R
/ActualText <FEFF004500780061006D0070006C0065003A00A00020002000200020002000730074006100740069006300200069006E007400200073007400610074006900630056006100720020003D002000310030003B0020002F002F00200053007400610074006900630020007600610072006900610062006C006500A0>
/Alt ()
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
435 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 809 0 R
/ActualText (Stack Dynamic Instances)
/Alt ()
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
436 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 810 0 R
/ActualText (Definition: Memory for stack dynamic instances is allocated on the stack at runtime, typically when a function is called, and deallocated when the function exits.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
437 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 811 0 R
/ActualText (Scope: Limited to the block in which they are defined \(e.g., a function or a loop\).)
/Alt ()
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
438 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 812 0 R
/ActualText (Lifetime: Exists only while the function is executing, and is automatically cleaned up when the function returns.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
439 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 813 0 R
/ActualText (Example:)
/Alt ()
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
440 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 814 0 R
/ActualText <>
/Alt ()
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
441 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 815 0 R
/ActualText (There are several advantages of stack-dynamic local variables, the primary one being the flexibility they provide to the subprogram. It is essential that recursive subprograms have stack-dynamic local variables. Another advantage of stack-dynamic locals is that the storage for local variables in an active subprogram can be shared with the local variables in all inactive subprograms.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
442 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 816 0 R
/ActualText (The main disadvantages of stack-dynamic local variables are the following: First, there is the cost of the time required to allocate, initialize \(when neces- sary\), and deallocate such variables for each call to the subprogram. Second, accesses to stack-dynamic local variables must be indirect, whereas accesses to static variables can be direct.4 This indirectness is required because the place in the stack where a particular local variable will reside can be determined only during execution )
/Alt ()
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
443 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 817 0 R
/ActualText <>
/Alt ()
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
444 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 818 0 R
/ActualText ( The greatest disadvantage of static local variables is their inability to support recursion. Also, their storage cannot be shared with the local variables of other inactive subprograms.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
445 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 819 0 R
/ActualText (In most contemporary languages, local variables in a subprogram are by default stack dynamic. In C and C++ functions, locals are stack dynamic unless specifically declared to be static. For example, in the following C \(or C++\) function, the variable sum is static and count is stack dynamic.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
446 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (int adder\(int list[], int listlen\) { static int sum = 0; int count; for \(count = 0; count < listlen; count ++\) sum += list [count]; return sum; })
/E (int adder\(int list[], int listlen\) { static int sum = 0; int count; for \(count = 0; count < listlen; count ++\) sum += list [count]; return sum; })
/Alt ()
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
447 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 820 0 R
/ActualText (The methods of C++, Java, and C# have only stack-dynamic local variables. )
/Alt ()
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
448 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 821 0 R
/ActualText (In Python, the only declarations used in method definitions are for globals. Any variable declared to be global in a method must be a variable defined outside the method. A variable defined outside the method can be referenced in the method without declaring it to be global, but such a vari- able cannot be assigned in the method. If the name of a global variable is assigned in a method, it is implicitly declared to be a local and the assign- ment does not disturb the global. All local variables in Python methods are stack dynamic.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
