568 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 846 0 R
/ActualText ( Ada and Fortran 95 + allow the programmer to specify in mode, out mode, or inout mode on each formal parameter.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
569 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 847 0 R
/ActualText (The default parameter-passing method of C# is pass-by- value. Pass-by-reference can be specified by preceding both a for- mal parameter and its corresponding actual parameter with ref.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
570 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 848 0 R
/ActualText (C# also supports out-mode parameters, which are pass-by-reference parametersthat do not need initial values.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
571 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 849 0 R
/ActualText (The parameter-passing method of Python and Ruby is called pass-by- assignment. Because all data values are objects, every variable is a reference to an object. In pass-by-assignment, the actual parameter value is assigned to the formal parameter. Therefore, pass-by-assignment is in effect pass-by-reference, because the value of all actual parameters are references. )
/Alt ()
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
572 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (consider the following program fragment: var a,b : integer; procedure G\(c,d: integer\); begin     c:=c-d;     d:=c+d;     c:=d-c end; a:=2; b:=3; G\(a,b\); If both parameters to G are passed by reference, what are the values of a and b at the end of the above program fragment ?)
/E (consider the following program fragment: var a,b : integer; procedure G\(c,d: integer\); begin     c:=c-d;     d:=c+d;     c:=d-c end; a:=2; b:=3; G\(a,b\); If both parameters to G are passed by reference, what are the values of a and b at the end of the above program fragment ?)
/Alt ()
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
